  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "A circle of radius $r$ rests inside a parabola with equation $y=x^2$, and only touches the bottom-most point $(0,0)$. What is the largest possible value of $r$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$r=\\frac12$.\n\nWith centre $(0,r)$, every other point of the parabola must lie outside the circle.",
      "worked": "The circle has centre $(0,r)$ and equation $x^2+(y-r)^2=r^2$. For a point $(x,x^2)$ on the parabola, its squared distance $d^2$ from the centre satisfies\n$$\\begin{align*}d^2-r^2&=x^2+(x^2-r)^2-r^2\\\\d^2-r^2&=x^4+(1-2r)x^2\\\\d^2-r^2&=x^2(x^2+1-2r).\\end{align*}$$\nFor the circle to remain inside the parabola, this must be positive for every $x\\ne0$. If $r>\\frac12$, it is negative for sufficiently small nonzero $x$, so the circle crosses the parabola.\nAt $r=\\frac12$,\n$$d^2-r^2=x^4>0\\qquad(x\\ne0).$$\nThus the only contact is at the origin, and the largest radius is $\\boxed{\\frac12}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
